									"text" : "This code is run for every cell, and should return the new state of the cell (in the typical 1-bit case: 1 if the cell will be alive at the next generation, 0 otherwise).\nKnowing that a t_cell is defined as \"typedef unsigned char t_cell;\", some variables can be used inside the code:\n• \"cell\": a t_cell containing the current state of the cell;\n• \"neighbors_size\": an int containing the neighbors size; \n• \"neighbors\": an double array of neighbors_size by neighbors_size cells containing each a t_cell element; \n• \"sum_neighbors\": the sum of all the value of the neighbors of the current cell;\n• \"nonzero_neighbors: the number of all the non-zero neighbors of the current cell.\n\nMore precisely, the prototype of the function being written is thus:\nt_cell life_customstep_one(t_cell cell, int neighbors_size, t_cell **neighbors, int sum_neighbors, int nonzero_neighbors);\nThis code is compiled on the fly, so that users can take advantage of native speed for complex rules.",
									"textcolor" : [ 0.501961, 0.501961, 0.501961, 1.0 ]
								}

							}
